From bf7f0a38c18a395d4a288a70d0fc36245e736077 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 14:31:34 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/6] fix(ci): untrack the delivery patch that has been failing
 every build on main
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

`node scripts/check-repo-hygiene.mjs` exits 1 on main because
FLOORFORGE_46_database_not_configured.patch is tracked at the repository
root. It is step 2 of 8 in .github/workflows/verify.yml, so typecheck,
lint, build and the audit scripts have not run on main since that file
landed.

The workflow's own header comment says this gate exists because "a
delivery patch file reached `main` twice through the GitHub web UI". It
reached main a third time, and the gate built to catch it became the
reason nothing else was being checked. A red gate protects nothing; it
teaches the team to stop reading the build.

.gitignore:45 already lists `*.patch`. That rule cannot help here — the
GitHub web UI force-adds, and .gitignore only governs untracked files.
The hygiene script is the only defence, which is why it has to be green.

For the record, both gates behind it pass on this base: `npx tsc
--noEmit` is clean and `npx eslint` reports one warning, zero errors.
The damage was never a hidden type error. It was that nobody could know.

