-FLOORFORGE_46 — a missing database is a known state, not a server fault.
-
-WHERE THIS CAME FROM
-
-Not from a checklist. It fell out of the audit run: four identical stack traces
-at the end of otherwise clean output.
-
-    GET /api/applications error: Error: Failed to fetch pilot applications:
-      TypeError: fetch failed
-        at Module.r7 [as getPilotApplications] ...
-
-WHAT WAS ACTUALLY HAPPENING
-
-lib/db/client.ts already KNEW the credentials were absent — it logs a warning at
-module load — and then did nothing with that knowledge. It substituted a
-placeholder host:
-
-    const resolvedSupabaseUrl = supabaseUrl || "https://example.supabase.co";
-
-and let every request go out to a domain FloorForge does not own. The fetch
-fails, and five route files turned that into:
-
-    500  {"error":{"code":"INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR",
-          "message":"Failed to fetch pilot applications: TypeError: fetch failed"}}
-
-Measured before the fix, every one of these returned 500 with that body:
-/api/applications GET+POST, /api/applications/[id] GET+PATCH, /api/jobs GET+POST,
-/api/jobs/[id] GET+PATCH, /api/telemetry POST.
-
-WHY IT MATTERS, IN INCREASING ORDER OF COST
-
-1. A CONFIGURATION STATE REPORTED AS A SERVER FAULT. 500 means the server broke.
-   Missing credentials on a pre-launch site is not a fault — it is the expected
-   state, and it already has a status code: 503.
-
-2. INTERNAL EXCEPTION TEXT RETURNED TO THE CALLER. Nine handlers echoed
-   `error.message` into the response body. Today that string is harmless. The
-   day it contains a hostname, a table name or a connection detail it is not,
-   and nobody will have changed anything for it to become a leak.
-
-3. THE EXPENSIVE ONE. The moment you set the Supabase credentials, a genuine
-   outage produces the IDENTICAL status, the IDENTICAL body and the IDENTICAL
-   log line as today's expected state. The signal everyone has already learned
-   to scroll past becomes indistinguishable from "the pilot pipeline is down and
-   leads are being lost right now". That is how an outage runs for days.
-
-THE FIX
-
-  lib/db/configured.ts (new)
-    DATABASE_CONFIGURED       one honest check, no placeholder host
-    DATABASE_NOT_CONFIGURED   a distinct code a monitor can ignore separately
-                              from a fault it should page on
-    databaseNotConfigured()   503 + Retry-After: 3600 + Cache-Control: no-store,
-                              and a body that says what is true: "not configured
-                              on this deployment... part of the pilot backend...
-                              No data was read or written."
-    internalError()           logs the real error in full server-side, returns
-                              a generic message to the client
-
-  All five route files guard before touching the database, so nothing leaves for
-  a host nobody owns and no stack trace is generated for a condition that is not
-  an error. The two 404 handlers stopped echoing the exception as well — a
-  lookup miss should say "Job not found", not whatever the data layer said.
-
-MEASURED AFTER
-
-    GET  /api/applications  -> 503  DATABASE_NOT_CONFIGURED
-    GET  /api/jobs          -> 503  DATABASE_NOT_CONFIGURED
-    POST /api/telemetry     -> 503  DATABASE_NOT_CONFIGURED
-    stack traces in the server log during those calls: 0
-
-WHAT DELIBERATELY DID NOT CHANGE
-
-The waitlist. Its ladder is POST /api/applications -> Formspree -> a prefilled
-mailto, and lib/waitlist.ts treats anything that is not 201 or 400 as
-"unavailable" and falls through. 503 degrades exactly as 500 did. verify46
-drives the real form in a browser and confirms a prospect still reaches "Your
-email is drafted" — the wording that refuses to claim they are on a list they
-are not on — with no internal error shown and no uncaught page error.
-
-No visible copy, no disclaimer, no schema, no public route changed.
-
-VERIFIED against a production build served on :3111
-
-  verify46          79/79 pass. Nine endpoints x (503 + distinct code); the
-                    message names the condition, frames it as pre-launch and
-                    states nothing was touched; Retry-After and no-store set;
-                    and every endpoint checked against five leak patterns
-                    (TypeError, "fetch failed", supabase.co, "at Module.",
-                    ".next/server") — 45 leak assertions, all clean.
-  axe-core          0 violations across 12 routes
-  contrast          0 FAIL          token-contrast  37 pairs, 0 FAIL
-  structure         clean           tap-targets     clean
-  input-font-size   clean           parse-check     clean
-  stress.mjs        133 passed, 0 failed against the local build
-  verifyapplied.sh  136 in, 0 out (9 new assertions)
-  tsc --noEmit      clean
-  eslint            0 errors (1 pre-existing warning, lib/db/client.ts:172)
-  next build        21/21 routes
-
-WHAT THIS BUYS YOU THE DAY YOU ADD CREDENTIALS
-
-Right now every one of these endpoints answers 503 DATABASE_NOT_CONFIGURED. The
-moment NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL and NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY are set, they
-start answering for real — and if Supabase is ever down after that, the response
-is a 500 with a code that means something, against a baseline of 503 that means
-something else. You can alert on the difference. You could not before.
-
-STILL NOT FIXED, AND STILL YOURS
-
-lib/db/client.ts:19 keeps the `https://example.supabase.co` placeholder so the
-module can be imported without credentials. Nothing reaches it now — every route
-returns before the client is used — but the cleaner shape is a client that is
-null until configured. That is a refactor of the data layer, not a rider on an
-error-handling patch.
-
-NOTE ON ORDER
-
-Applies on top of FLOORFORGE_45.
